# One line ending everywhere: LF in git, LF in the working tree.
#
# Sources are edited on Windows and deployed to a Linux Zabbix frontend, and the two
# had drifted apart: js_wrapper/*.php, *.js and *.css were stored as CRLF while the
# markdown and the built UMD assets next to them were LF, and two files under
# example_UMD_module/ held both endings inside one file.
#
# Nothing was broken by it - the repository holds no shell script and no shebang, and
# PHP, JS, CSS and JSON do not care. The cost was diffs: rewriting one line with an
# editor that disagrees turns into a whole-file change, which happened here.
#
# text=auto leaves git to detect binaries; eol=lf pins the working tree as well, so
# the mixture cannot creep back in.
* text=auto eol=lf
